        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The STS-76 Shuttle mission was the third docking between a U.S. Space Shuttle and the Russian Mir Space Station. The Shuttle crew included Commander Kevin P. Chilton, Pilot Richard A. Searfoss, and Mission Specialists Shannon W. Lucid, Linda M. Godwin, Michael R. Cliffod and Ronald M. Sega.  The main objective for the STS-76 crew was to transport U.S. astronaut Shannon Lucid to the Mir Space Station to work as a Mir crewmember.  She lived and worked with the Russian cosmonauts for over six months, a record for U.S. duration in space and for a female crewmember throughout the world.  Her primary responsibility during her record-breaking stay was to perform research and manage payload operations.<P>The flight of STS-76 also fulfilled another important objective; it provided hardware and resupply materials for experiments to be performed aboard Mir and returned experiment samples and data to Earth.  The Shuttle docked to the Mir Station for 5 days, as the U.S. astronauts and the Russian cosmonauts worked together to complete the transfer of supplies and equipment between the Shuttle and the Mir.  In the payload bay, the Shuttle carried two special additions:  the Spacehab, a small laboratory module, and the Mir Docking module.  An Extravehicular Activity (EVA) by astronauts Michael Clifford and Linda Godwin on the fourth day of flight served to place and activate four experiments on the external surface of the Mir Docking Module.  The experiments, collectively referred to as the Mir Environmental Effects Payload (MEEP), were designed to help characterize the space environment at a 51.6 degree inclination, the same inclination at which the International Space Station is to be built.  <P>Godwin and Clifford also worked with common U.S. and Russian EVA hardware such as safety tethers and foot restraints to retrieve a video camera mounted on Mir.  Their EVA represented one in a series aimed at testing equipment and procedures that may be implemented during assembly and maintenance of the International Space Station.<P>The majority of the experiments transported to Mir by STS-76 were performed after the Shuttle returned to Earth, during the NASA 2 mission. This allowed more time to conduct these experiments, since Mir is the only permanently manned space station currently in orbit. The Shuttle was also used to transport and return to Earth other hardware and consumables, including experiment data and samples from investigations conducted during the Mir 21 mission. Additional equipment was delivered at various times to the Mir Station by Russia's unmanned Progress resupply vehicle.<P>There were, however, areas of research in which investigations were performed only on the Shuttle.  Fundamental Biology investigators from NASA and the European Space Agency (ESA) worked on a series of experiments known as the Biorack experiments.  The Biorack experiments studied the effects of microgravity and cosmic radiation, particularly the effects of high-energy particles on the development of plants, tissues, cells, bacteria and insects during spaceflight. Developed by the ESA, the Biorack is a complete experiment rack that fits into the Spacehab module.  It consists of two incubators with 1-g reference centrifuges, a glovebox, and a refrigerator/freezer unit. Among the international set of Biorack investigations were a study of microgravity on bone loss; the effects of using centrifuges as 1-g references for the microgravity tests; cell response, repair, activation, and gene expression in microgravity; developmental processes; plant experiments; and a dosimetry experiment to document the radiation environment inside the Biorack facility and other locations inside the Spacehab module and middeck.  The three U.S. investigations were as follows:<BR><LI>BIORACK: Microgravity Effects on Bone Cell Gene Expression (OSTEO)<LI>BIORACK: Mechanisms of Gravity Sensing and Response in Hematopoietic Cells (TCELL)<LI>BIORACK: Modification of Radiogenic Damage By Microgravity (ELEGANS)<P>In the area of human life sciences, two joint operations were conducted to test the quality of the air and water on the Mir space station. Since the Mir Space Station has been a closed system for over ten years, scientists wanted to perform detailed analyses on the air to determine if any volatile organic compounds (VOCs) exist. The air and water analyses will help scientists and engineers design safe and efficient air and water systems for the future international space station.<P>Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs) were also performed on this mission.  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation that is performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who serve as the test subjects.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.<P>Three joint microgravity experiments were performed on the STS-76 mission.  Two crystal growth experiments were initiated during the docked phase and transferred to the Mir station.  The third experiment measured Mir and Shuttle accelerations during the docked phase.  <P>Two payloads were flown on STS-76 to provide students the opportunity to participate with the mission.  A payload known as KidSat provided students in grades K-12 access to real-time images of the Earth from their own observing instruments in space.  The Shuttle Amateur Radio Experiment (SAREX) allowed students to talk with STS-76 crewmembers via ham radio.  During the communication sessions, students talked with the crew about mission activities and learned about living and working in space.<END>
